<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ming Xia</h1><p class="st-meta">1362 CE – 1371 CE · capital Chongqing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A rebel 'Great Xia' empire in Sichuan, founded by a Red Turban general and remembered for mild rule before its conquest by the Ming in 1371.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-yuzhen">Ming Yuzhen</a>, a commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shouhui">Xu Shouhui's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban</a> regime, led an army up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 1357 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a> murdered Xu in 1360, Ming Yuzhen refused to accept him, and in 1362 proclaimed himself emperor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-xia">Great Xia</a> at Chongqing.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-yuzhen">Ming Yuzhen</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> with comparatively light taxes, held civil service examinations on the Chinese model and promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turbans</a> while restraining other cults. Protected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> gorges, the state stayed out of the great wars in the lower valley, though an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> failed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-yuzhen">Ming Yuzhen</a> died in 1366, leaving his ten-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-sheng">Ming Sheng</a> under regents. In 1371 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> armies under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> He and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-youde">Fu Youde</a> advanced by river and over the mountains; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> surrendered, and Ming Sheng was sent into exile in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ming-xia.jpg" alt="Ming Xia" width="320" height="276"><figcaption>Image: Joowwww; derivative work by Rowanwindwhistler work: Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cina_roku_1365-es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1362 CE – 1371 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chongqing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ming Yuzhen, Ming Sheng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maitreya Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xia (Ming Yuzhen), Great Xia (1362-1371)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1366&amp;focus=ming-xia" aria-label="Ming Xia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7453 1098 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ming Xia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7773 1354L7867 1005M7773 1354L7643 1242"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7867 1005h0M7643 1242h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7773 1354h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7773 1354h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chongqing. <a href="/atlas?year=1366&amp;focus=ming-xia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
